![](/images/articles/hero-2.jpeg)

In[ my last post](https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/we-dont-need-another-hero-part-1-steve-bamberger/), I described how hero-driven organizations fail when the business exceeds the capacity of the heroes.  Many startups recognize the need to scale, and can intellectually grasp the fragility of the hero model. But they are often surprised by how quickly and catastrophically those models collapse.

I have lost count of the business leaders who have told me that what they need are “scalable processes.” But what does that mean? These are some of the keys to scaling a go-to-market organization.

### Enablement

Scaling requires people. And people are a waste of money and talent (and a drain on morale and employer brand) unless you have the tools to make them successful. I made my first hire for sales enablement in 1999. (I was into sales enablement before it was cool.)

A well-run enablement function onboards your people in a structured, disciplined way. It gives you a content management infrastructure and a learning program. It helps your employees develop knowledge and skills, and provides a foundation for employee growth and promotion.

When you have outgrown your heroes, enablement puts everyone else to work.

### Specialization

Small companies value people who have the drive and the interest to work beyond their job description. They value people who “pitch in wherever needed” or are “versatile” or “utility players” or "wear many hats." I love versatile generalists, probably because I am one.  But there is a point at which an entire company of generalists break down.

Most small companies do not rigorously define processes to get tasks done across multiple people. A single customer success person may help a customer with implementation, then with training and adoption, then with an updated security review, and then with renewal paperwork. Similarly, a salesperson will make cold calls, evangelize at shows, advance deals, negotiate contracts, write business cases, support the customer post-sale, handle renewals, and farm existing accounts. Because there are no processes to define how these functions integrate, it is incredibly difficult to onboard new employees to fulfill a single function.

But that puts a huge strain on hiring. It is difficult to hire *anyone* unless that person is good at a *lot* of things. There are not many salespeople who are good at both hunting and farming, and fewer who can also evangelize, prospect, negotiate, build relationships, and close. There are not many marketers who are good at branding, content, product, competitive, events, and demand gen. There are not many customer success people who can handle the technical, business, relationship, and commercial aspects of a customer.

But because young companies are built on heroes, not processes, they become dependent on what baseball scouts call a “[five tool player](https://web.archive.org/web/20070825001942/http://www.sportingnews.com/archives/sports2000/players/151063.html).” They narrow their hiring pool to rare and expensive superstars instead of building a complete team. Ironically, what started as a response to a scale constraint (hiring one person to do multiple jobs) becomes a scaling liability (hiring a whole team of the rare people who can do everything).

Leaders must define the work that needs to get done and the skills required for each element of the customer journey. Once these are defined, you can refine the processes that unify the go-to-market organization, and recruit people with the right combination of skills. Be cautious in over-innovating on job definitions, because it makes it harder to find people experienced at doing the work you need done.

### Balance

A common mistake is that if you need more sales, just hire more salespeople.

Don't hire account executives that you can't afford to support and feed. Unless you're maintaining a reasonable manager span of control, sustaining your solution consultant to AE ratio, investing in enablement, and building pipeline effectively, why would you hire more salespeople? That's like ordering an extra entree for the table and forgetting to pay the tax and tip. You will starve your reps and create flight risks.

Instead, make sure that you hire in balance, with (on average) one non-AE hire for each AE you bring on board in the sales team. And be wary of hiring extra AEs without the demand generation engine to feed them.

### Standardization and Measurement

You need reliable processes and playbooks in place for every day situations, and escalation mechanisms for the unusual. You need structure, measurements, and metrics in place so you can see problems before they develop. The whole team needs to know exactly what defines a "stage 2" opportunity, and what they need to do to support those deals and each other.

* * * * * * *

When you build your organization to scale, you'll be able to put your heroes to work innovating in new business areas and developing the rest of the team. Your heroes will be contributing to your growth instead of holding you back.
